# Europe batch 1 (1440-1602): every brief rewritten to rules zero to four.
# bluf = purpose, outcome, consequence. now = the political or social consequence. Actors carry role and name on first mention.
OVERRIDE = True
A = {
"eu14400": dict(
 bluf="Mainz goldsmith Johannes Gutenberg set out to make a profit copying texts by machine. He got a working press and lost it to his creditor; the method spread to 250 towns within fifty years. Cheap, identical copies took control of information away from Church and crown, and the Reformation, the scientific journal and the standard national language all ran on it.",
 now="Print broke the clergy's monopoly on knowledge and gave rulers a new problem, a public that read. Every state answer since, from the licensing of presses to the regulation of social media, descends from the censorship laws of the 1520s.",
 story="Europe in 1440 copied books by hand at a few pages a day. Mainz goldsmith Johannes Gutenberg combined cast metal type, oil-based ink and a screw press adapted from the wine trade. His Bible came off the press around 1455. By 1500 presses ran in more than 250 towns and had printed some twenty million books.",
 why="Paper had been cheap since the 1300s. Towns held a paying market of merchants, lawyers, clergy and students. Goldsmiths knew how to cast metal to fine tolerances. Gutenberg assembled parts that already existed.",
 then="Identical copies let readers compare and correct one another's texts. Pamphlets reached readers faster than any bishop could answer them, which is how Martin Luther's case spread in 1517. Rulers and the Church replied with licensing and the Index of Forbidden Books of 1559.",
 fact="About 180 Gutenberg Bibles were printed; around 49 survive."),
"eu14531": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II wanted the city that split his European and Asian lands and the straits it commanded. He took it in 53 days with the largest guns yet cast, ending the Roman state after eleven centuries. The Ottomans gained the capital of a three-continent empire, and every fortress in Europe became obsolete.",
 now="The Ottomans ruled the Balkans and the Arab lands from the city for four centuries. Turkey still holds the straits between the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, the lever in every Russian crisis from 1853 to 2022.",
 story="By 1453 the Byzantine Empire was the city and little else. Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II, aged 21, besieged it in April with about 80,000 men and bombards that broke the land walls. Byzantine Emperor Constantine XI died in the final assault on 29 May. The city became the Ottoman capital.",
 why="The Fourth Crusade's sack of 1204 had left Byzantium a shell. A century of Ottoman conquest in Anatolia and the Balkans had surrounded it. The city sat astride the straits between the empire's two halves.",
 trig="Mehmed built the fortress of Rumeli Hisarı on the Bosporus in 1452 to cut the city off by sea.",
 then="The sultans claimed the Roman inheritance and pushed into the Balkans, the Arab lands and the Mediterranean. The Orthodox patriarchate survived under Ottoman protection. Moscow later called itself the Third Rome.",
 myth="The fall closed the spice routes and forced Europeans to sail round Africa. Portugal was already working down the African coast, and the Ottomans taxed the eastern trade rather than stopping it.",
 fact="Mehmed had his galleys dragged overland on greased logs into the Golden Horn to get past the harbour chain."),
"eu14532": dict(
 bluf="English kings fought for 116 years to hold or take the French crown. French King Charles VII drove them out with a standing army and field artillery, leaving England only Calais. France gained a king who could tax and raise troops without consent, the base of absolutism; England turned inward to civil war and later to the sea.",
 now="The war gave France a permanent army and a permanent tax, and England a parliament that had learned to bargain over war money. Both kingdoms came out with a national identity defined against the other.",
 story="English kings had held French lands as vassals since 1066. English King Edward III claimed the French crown in 1337, and the war ran on and off for 116 years. Joan of Arc broke the siege of Orléans in 1429. French cannon won the last battle at Castillon in 1453.",
 why="Disputed lordship over Gascony came first. The claim to the crown through Edward III's mother gave it a dynastic form. The Flemish wool trade and France's alliance with Scotland kept both sides in.",
 trig="French victories at Formigny in 1450 and Castillon in 1453 drove the English out of Normandy and Gascony.",
 then="The French crown kept the taille, a tax raised without an assembly, and the ordinance companies, a standing army. England lost all but Calais, fell into the Wars of the Roses within two years and later turned to naval power.",
 fact="Castillon was among the first battles decided mainly by field artillery."),
"eu14553": dict(
 bluf="Two branches of the English royal family fought for the crown for thirty years. The fighting killed off much of the old nobility and ended with a Tudor on the throne. The winner, English King Henry VII, built a monarchy strong enough to break with Rome fifty years later.",
 now="The wars destroyed the private armies of the magnates. The Tudor state that replaced them governed through royal officials and Parliament, the structure that carried England through the Reformation.",
 story="English King Henry VI lost his mind in 1453 and his grip on the nobility with it. Richard, Duke of York, claimed the right to govern. The first battle at St Albans in May 1455 opened thirty years of intermittent civil war between the houses of Lancaster and York.",
 why="Defeat in France discredited the king's government and brought home nobles with armed retinues. A king who could not govern left rival magnates to fight for control of the court.",
 trig="Richard, Duke of York, took up arms against the king's favourites at St Albans in May 1455.",
 then="Many noble lines died out or lost their lands. After 1485 Henry VII banned private armies, rebuilt the royal finances and ruled through councils of professional officials.",
 fact="The red and white roses were adopted as symbols long after; Shakespeare's history plays fixed the wars in popular memory."),
"d-gutbible": dict(
 bluf="Johannes Gutenberg needed a prestige product to repay his loans, and a Latin Bible had a guaranteed market in churches and monasteries. The book matched the finest manuscripts and proved printing could pay. Investors across Europe backed presses on that proof, and printing spread within a generation.",
 now="The Bible showed that print could serve the Church as well as undermine it. Within seventy years the same technology carried Luther's attack on the Church that had bought the first copies.",
 story="About 180 copies of the Latin Bible were finished in Mainz by 1455, a few on vellum, most on paper. Each page carried 42 lines in two columns, with initials added by hand. A future pope who saw sample pages in 1454 wrote that they could be read without spectacles.",
 why="Gutenberg had borrowed heavily from the Mainz financier Johann Fust. A Bible was the one book every religious house wanted and would pay for.",
 then="Fust sued in 1455 and took over much of the press with Gutenberg's assistant Peter Schöffer. Trained printers carried the method to other cities; by 1500 presses ran in more than 250 towns.",
 fact="Around 49 copies survive, the most valuable printed books in the world."),
"eu14694": dict(
 bluf="Isabella of Castile needed an ally to win a disputed succession; Ferdinand of Aragon wanted Castile's manpower against France. Their marriage joined the two largest Iberian kingdoms under one couple. The joint monarchy conquered Granada, founded the Inquisition, expelled the Jews and financed Columbus, and its grandson inherited a world empire.",
 now="Spain was built as a union of kingdoms that kept their own laws. The tension between Madrid and regions with their own identity, Catalonia above all, descends from that founding bargain.",
 story="Castile faced a contested succession in 1469. Princess Isabella of Castile married Prince Ferdinand of Aragon in secret at Valladolid, against the wishes of her half-brother, Castilian King Henry IV. From 1479 the couple ruled both kingdoms as partners.",
 why="Isabella needed Aragonese support to beat a rival claimant. Aragon, a Mediterranean power, needed Castile's population and revenue against France.",
 then="The two crowns kept separate laws and parliaments but acted together in war, foreign policy and religion. Their children married into the Habsburgs; their grandson became Holy Roman Emperor Charles V in 1519.",
 fact="Isabella's marriage contract gave her the governing power in Castile; Ferdinand signed as her consort."),
"eu14785": dict(
 bluf="Spanish King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella wanted a royal court that could enforce one faith across two kingdoms. The Inquisition tried tens of thousands and burned a few thousand, most in its first decades, and tied Spanish identity to Catholic orthodoxy and purity of blood. It made religious conformity a function of the state for three centuries and drove the expulsions of 1492 and 1609.",
 now="The Inquisition set the principle that a subject's ancestry could make his loyalty suspect, a doctrine later racial states adopted. It kept Spain closed to the Reformation and much of the printed learning of the north.",
 story="Spain held a large class of converted Jews, the New Christians, who had risen in trade and office since the forced conversions of 1391. Spanish King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella obtained papal permission for a royal tribunal in 1478. Under Grand Inquisitor Tomás de Torquemada it investigated, tortured and burned those it judged secret Jews.",
 why="Resentment of successful converts gave the court a constituency. The monarchs wanted an institution answerable to the crown rather than Rome. Religious unity was their chosen basis for governing two separate kingdoms.",
 then="The court pressed for the expulsion of the Jews in 1492 and of the Moriscos in 1609. Purity-of-blood statutes barred people of Jewish or Muslim descent from many offices. The Inquisition was abolished in 1834.",
 fact="Its most active years were its first two decades; in the 1700s it tried a few dozen cases a year."),
"d-otranto": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II sent a force to Italy to open a road to Rome. His commander took Otranto and held it a year; Mehmed's death in 1481 ended the campaign. The scare united the Italian states briefly and fixed the fear of Ottoman invasion in papal politics for a century.",
 now="Otranto showed that the Ottomans could land in Italy and that Italy's divided states could barely answer. Later sultans chose naval power and North African bases over invasion, which set the Mediterranean contest of the 1500s.",
 story="Italy's states were at war with each other in 1480. Ottoman commander Gedik Ahmed Pasha landed in Apulia in July and took Otranto on 11 August after a two-week siege. About 800 townsmen who refused to convert were executed. Ottoman Sultan Mehmed II died in May 1481; the garrison surrendered in September.",
 why="Mehmed saw himself as heir to Rome and had conquered the Balkans. Italy's feuding states offered no united defence.",
 then="Neapolitan forces retook the town in 1481. The Ottomans pursued the Mediterranean through their fleet and the corsairs of North Africa rather than through Italy.",
 fact="The 800 executed townsmen were canonised as the Martyrs of Otranto in 2013."),
"eu14856": dict(
 bluf="Henry Tudor, Earl of Richmond, invaded with French backing to take a crown held by a usurper. He killed English King Richard III at Bosworth and married the Yorkist heiress to end the civil wars. The Tudor monarchy he founded centralised the state, broke with Rome and built the navy that made England a sea power.",
 now="The Tudors gave England a national church, a navy and a royal administration that outlived the dynasty. The national story Shakespeare told was written to justify their victory.",
 story="English King Richard III had taken the throne in 1483; his two young nephews had disappeared in the Tower. Henry Tudor, Earl of Richmond, landed in Wales with French-financed troops. At Bosworth Field in August 1485 the Stanley family changed sides, and Richard died fighting. Henry VII married Elizabeth of York, joining the two houses.",
 why="Richard's seizure of the throne and the disappearance of the princes turned nobles against him. Henry was the leading surviving Lancastrian claimant and had French money.",
 then="Henry VII rebuilt the royal finances, banned private armies and governed through councils of lawyers and clerks. His son Henry VIII and granddaughter Elizabeth I used that state to break with Rome and fight Spain.",
 fact="Richard III's bones were found under a Leicester car park in 2012."),
"eu14927": dict(
 bluf="Spanish King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella set out to finish the Christian conquest of Iberia. After a ten-year war the last emir surrendered Granada and the Alhambra on 2 January 1492. The monarchs then turned the crown's resources to religious uniformity and overseas ventures; Columbus sailed within the year.",
 now="The fall of Granada completed a Christian monarchy that then expelled its Jews, converted its Muslims by force and financed Columbus the same year; the Spanish state was built on religious uniformity and overseas conquest from its first months.",
 story="The Nasrid emirate of Granada had survived two centuries as a tributary of Castile. Spanish King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella began the conquest in 1482. Emir Muhammad XII, whom Spaniards called Boabdil, surrendered the city on 2 January 1492 on terms that promised Muslims freedom of worship.",
 why="Feuds inside the Nasrid family split the emirate. The union of Castile and Aragon gave the Christians the men and money for a ten-year war of sieges.",
 then="Forced conversions began in 1499 and the surrender terms were dead by 1502. Three months after the surrender the monarchs agreed to fund Columbus. The converted Moriscos were expelled in 1609-14.",
 fact="The Alhambra and the Arabic place names of Andalusia are what remain of eight centuries of Islamic rule."),
"eu14927b": dict(
 bluf="Spanish King Ferdinand and Queen Isabella ordered the Jews of Spain to convert or leave within four months, to make one faith the basis of their joint rule. Tens of thousands left for Portugal, North Africa, Italy and the Ottoman Empire. Spain lost its physicians, financiers and artisans, and the Sephardic diaspora carried Spanish trade networks across the Mediterranean.",
 now="The expulsion set the principle of one realm, one faith, which the Peace of Augsburg made the European rule in 1555. The Sephardic communities it created built the trade networks of Amsterdam, Livorno and Salonica.",
 story="The Alhambra Decree of 31 March 1492 gave Spain's Jews until the end of July to convert or leave. Estimates of those who left run from 40,000 to over 100,000; many others converted and lived under the Inquisition's suspicion.",
 why="The Inquisition argued that practising Jews were drawing converts back to Judaism. With Granada taken, the monarchs made religious uniformity the basis of their rule.",
 then="Portugal forced its Jews to convert in 1497. Ottoman Sultan Bayezid II welcomed the refugees to Salonica and Istanbul. Sephardic merchants later built trading houses in Amsterdam, London and the Americas.",
 fact="Spain and Portugal passed citizenship laws for Sephardic descendants in 2015."),
"eu14948": dict(
 bluf="French King Charles VIII marched into Italy to claim the kingdom of Naples. He took it in five months, and every other power came in to stop him. Sixty-five years of war ended Italian independence under Spanish control and produced the balance of power, the resident ambassador and the modern fortress.",
 now="Italy stayed divided and under foreign influence until the 1860s. The balance-of-power system that Europe practised until 1914 was learned in these wars.",
 story="Italy in 1494 was five major states at war with each other. French King Charles VIII crossed the Alps with a siege train of modern bronze cannon and took Naples in February 1495. Venice, Milan, the Pope, Spain and the Emperor formed a league to drive him out. The peninsula was Europe's battlefield until 1559.",
 why="France and Spain, newly consolidated, held rival claims to Naples and Milan. Italy's wealth invited intervention and its divisions made it possible.",
 trig="Milanese ruler Ludovico Sforza invited Charles VIII to press his claim to Naples in 1494.",
 then="Spain held Naples and Milan by the Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis in 1559. Italian states invented the resident ambassador to manage alliances. The bastioned fortress spread from Italy across Europe.",
 fact="Charles VIII's cannon reduced in hours castles that had held for months against medieval engines."),
"d-aldine": dict(
 bluf="Venetian printer Aldus Manutius set out to print accurate Greek and Latin texts in formats a reader could carry. He succeeded, with italic type and the small octavo. Classical learning became a shared European possession, and private reading away from the library became ordinary.",
 now="Aldus made the book a personal object. The reading public that the Reformation and the Enlightenment relied on grew from his portable, affordable editions.",
 story="Venice was the printing capital of Europe. Venetian printer Aldus Manutius founded his press around 1494 to issue edited texts of Aristotle and other Greek authors. From 1501 he printed small octavo classics in a new slanted type, italic, which packed more words on the page.",
 why="Venice had capital, trade routes and Greek refugees from the Ottomans who had brought their manuscripts. Demand for reliable texts was rising among the humanists.",
 then="Aldine editions of Aristotle, Plato and the Latin poets spread humanist scholarship across Europe. The small format let people read anywhere.",
 fact="Italic type and the semicolon in its modern use both come from his press."),
"eu15039": dict(
 bluf="Florentine painter Leonardo da Vinci took a merchant's commission for a portrait of his wife and kept the painting for the rest of his life. He carried it to the French court, and it has belonged to the French state since. The painting shows the Renaissance shift toward secular subjects, the individual face and techniques grounded in the study of nature.",
 now="The painting marks the moment European art became an instrument of courts and states: Leonardo took it to the French king, and it has been a possession of the French state since, used in diplomacy and as a draw for Paris.",
 story="Florentine merchant families commissioned portraits to display status. Florentine painter Leonardo da Vinci painted Lisa Gherardini, wife of the silk merchant Francesco del Giocondo, using sfumato, a soft blending of tones. He worked on it for years and never delivered it.",
 why="Leonardo's study of anatomy and optics let him paint lifelike transitions of light. The merchant market for portraits paid for the experiment.",
 then="The half-length figure turned three-quarters against a landscape became the model for European portraiture. The painting passed to French King Francis I and, after the Revolution, to the Louvre as public property.",
 fact="Its global celebrity dates from its theft in 1911."),
"eu150810": dict(
 bluf="Pope Julius II wanted a chapel ceiling that would proclaim the restored power of the papacy. Florentine sculptor Michelangelo gave him more than 300 figures in four years. The spending that paid for such work, and for the new St Peter's, was financed partly by indulgence sales, which provoked Luther within five years.",
 now="The Renaissance papacy acted as an Italian prince, building and warring on borrowed money. That conduct, and the indulgences sold to cover it, supplied the Reformation's first grievance.",
 story="Pope Julius II, who led armies in person, hired Florentine sculptor Michelangelo in 1508 to paint the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel. Michelangelo, who regarded himself as a sculptor, covered it with scenes from Genesis, including the Creation of Adam, and finished in 1512.",
 why="Julius aimed to restore papal authority in Italy and make Rome the capital of Christendom in stone and paint.",
 then="The ceiling raised the artist's standing from craftsman to genius. Rebuilding St Peter's, begun under Julius in 1506, was financed through indulgence sales that Luther attacked in 1517.",
 fact="Michelangelo painted standing on scaffolding, head tilted back; the legend that he lay on his back is false."),
"eu151311": dict(
 bluf="Florentine diplomat Niccolò Machiavelli wrote to win back office from the Medici and to show how a prince could hold a state after foreign armies had overrun Italy. The book failed to win him a post and was banned in 1559. It founded the practice of analysing power as it is used rather than as it ought to be, the realist tradition in politics.",
 now="Realism in international relations, the view that states pursue power and security before morality, descends from the book. Every ruler since has been read with its questions in mind.",
 story="Florentine diplomat Niccolò Machiavelli served the Florentine republic for fourteen years and watched French and Spanish armies overrun Italy. Dismissed and tortured when the Medici returned in 1512, he wrote 'The Prince' to show how a new ruler could seize, hold and defend a state.",
 why="Italy's defeats taught him that moral rules protected no state. He wanted office and wrote for the Medici.",
 then="The Church put the book on the Index in 1559. Rulers and ministers read it anyway. His 'Discourses on Livy', which praised republics, fed later republican thought.",
 fact="The book circulated in manuscript for nineteen years and was printed in 1532, five years after his death."),
"eu151712": dict(
 bluf="Wittenberg friar Martin Luther set out to stop the sale of indulgences and to put scripture above the Pope. The Church excommunicated him, German princes protected him, and print carried his case across Germany in weeks. The Western Church split for good, a century of religious war followed, and religious authority passed from Rome to princes and states.",
 now="The split broke the one authority that had arbitrated between European rulers, produced a century of religious war, and gave northern Europe state churches, mass literacy for Bible reading, and the habit of resisting authority on conscience; its political effect in Germany ran to 1990.",
 story="Dominican preacher Johann Tetzel was selling indulgences in Saxony to fund the new St Peter's in Rome. Wittenberg friar Martin Luther posted 95 theses against the trade in October 1517. He argued that salvation came by faith alone and that the Bible, rather than the Pope, was the final authority. Printers spread his theses across Germany within weeks.",
 why="German resentment of papal taxation came first. The empire's fragmentation let a prince shelter a dissident. Humanists had prepared readers for a return to the original texts, and print outran suppression.",
 trig="Johann Tetzel's indulgence campaign of 1517 reached the borders of Saxony.",
 then="Princes and cities that adopted Luther's teaching seized church lands and took control of religion in their territories, a principle the Peace of Augsburg fixed in 1555. The Western Church split into Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican branches. Religious war ran until 1648.",
 fact="Evangelical Protestantism is among the fastest-growing religious movements in Africa and Latin America today."),
"eu151913": dict(
 bluf="Charles of Habsburg bought the imperial election with Fugger loans to add the Holy Roman Empire to the Spanish, Burgundian and Austrian inheritances he already held. He won, and ruled more of Europe than anyone since Charlemagne. The empire proved too scattered to govern as one, and its wars with France, the Ottomans and the Protestants exhausted Spain and ended the ideal of a single Christian monarchy.",
 now="Charles's failure to hold Christendom together confirmed the sovereign state as Europe's unit. His division of the Habsburg lands in 1556 made Spain and Austria the two Catholic great powers for the next two centuries.",
 story="A chain of marriages and early deaths gave Charles of Habsburg the crowns of Castile and Aragon, the Low Countries, Austria and southern Italy by 1516. In 1519 he paid the seven German electors, with loans from the Augsburg banker Jakob Fugger, to elect him Holy Roman Emperor Charles V.",
 why="Habsburg marriage policy had collected the inheritances. French King Francis I bid for the same election, and the electors sold to the higher payer.",
 then="Charles fought France across Italy, held Vienna against the Ottomans in 1529 and failed to stop the Reformation. He abdicated in 1556 and split his lands between Spanish and Austrian branches. American silver paid for his wars and left Spain in debt.",
 fact="Charles was raised in Flanders and spoke no Spanish when he first arrived to rule Castile in 1517."),
"eu152114": dict(
 bluf="Holy Roman Emperor Charles V summoned Martin Luther to recant before the imperial assembly, to end the dispute by authority. Luther refused and was outlawed. The edict was never enforced, because the princes who protected him mattered more to Charles than one friar, and the Reformation survived on the empire's divisions.",
 now="Worms showed that a divided empire could not suppress dissent. The precedent that conscience can refuse an emperor became a founding text of religious and political freedom.",
 story="Holy Roman Emperor Charles V called Wittenberg friar Martin Luther before the Imperial Diet at Worms in April 1521 and ordered him to recant. Luther refused unless proven wrong by scripture. The Edict of Worms declared him an outlaw.",
 why="Charles wanted religious unity but needed the German princes against France and the Ottomans. He could not afford to crush Luther's protectors.",
 then="Elector Frederick the Wise of Saxony hid Luther at the Wartburg. The edict went unenforced, and princes and cities across the empire adopted Lutheranism through the 1520s.",
 fact="The words 'Here I stand, I can do no other' were added to the printed account; the record shows only 'God help me'."),
"d-lutherbible": dict(
 bluf="Martin Luther translated the New Testament into German so that laymen could read scripture without priests or Latin. The book sold out in months and ran through many editions. It gave the Reformation its engine, individual reading, and gave a politically divided people a common written language.",
 now="Luther's German became the standard written language of a country that had no state until 1871. Protestant regions required reading, and their literacy lead lasted for centuries.",
 story="Hidden at the Wartburg by Elector Frederick the Wise of Saxony, Wittenberg friar Martin Luther translated the New Testament from Greek in about eleven weeks. The September Testament of 1522 sold thousands of copies within months.",
 why="Luther held that every Christian should read the Bible directly. Print made a cheap vernacular Bible possible for the first time.",
 then="The complete Bible followed in 1534 and went through many editions in his lifetime. Protestant churches made reading a religious duty.",
 fact="Luther chose the chancery German of Saxony, which is why the dialect of one principality became the national language."),
"eu152615": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I marched north to break Hungary and reach the Habsburgs. He destroyed the Hungarian army in two hours and killed its king. Hungary ceased to exist as an independent state for 150 years, and the Ottoman and Habsburg empires faced each other across the Danube until 1699.",
 now="Mohács gave the Habsburgs the Hungarian crown and the role of defenders of Christian Europe, the justification for their empire until 1918. In Hungary the defeat remains the national catastrophe.",
 story="Hungary's nobles were feuding and the rest of Europe sent no help. On 29 August 1526 Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I's army crushed the Hungarians at Mohács in under two hours. Hungarian King Louis II drowned in a stream while fleeing; much of the nobility died on the field.",
 why="Suleiman sought Central Europe and the weakening of the Habsburgs. Hungary was divided and alone.",
 then="Louis II died without heirs, and Archduke Ferdinand of Habsburg claimed the throne. Hungary was split three ways for 150 years: Habsburg Royal Hungary, the Ottoman centre, and the Ottoman vassal Transylvania.",
 fact="Hungarians still say 'more was lost at Mohács' to put a misfortune in proportion."),
"eu152916": dict(
 bluf="Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I marched on Vienna to drive Archduke Ferdinand of Habsburg out of Hungary. Rain cost him his heavy guns and the season ran out; he withdrew in October. The Ottoman frontier stopped in central Hungary for 150 years, and the Habsburgs became the defenders of Christian Europe.",
 now="The failed siege fixed the Ottoman frontier in Hungary for 150 years and made the Habsburgs the defenders of Christian Europe, the role that justified their empire until 1918.",
 story="Ottoman Sultan Suleiman I reached Vienna in late September 1529 with a large army but without the heavy cannon abandoned in the mud on the march. The garrison held against mines and assaults. In mid-October, with winter coming, the Ottomans withdrew.",
 why="Suleiman backed his vassal John Zápolya for the Hungarian crown against Ferdinand. Vienna lay at the limit of what an army from Istanbul could reach and fight in one season.",
 then="The Ottomans held central Hungary; Vienna stayed Habsburg. The two empires settled into a frontier war; the Habsburgs built a fortified military border.",
 fact="The sieges of 1529 and 1683 are still invoked in arguments about Europe's borders and identity."),
"eu153417": dict(
 bluf="English King Henry VIII wanted a male heir and an annulment the Pope would not grant. Parliament made him head of the English church in 1534, and he closed the monasteries and took their wealth. Religion became a matter of national sovereignty, the gentry who bought church land got a stake in the break, and England's Protestant identity set its rivalries for three centuries.",
 now="The crown still heads the Church of England. Anti-Catholicism shaped British identity and the Irish conflict, and the monastic lands created the landed class that governed England until the 1900s.",
 story="English King Henry VIII had no son by Catherine of Aragon, and Pope Clement VII would not annul the marriage; Catherine's nephew, Holy Roman Emperor Charles V, controlled Rome. Henry married Anne Boleyn in 1533. Parliament's Act of Supremacy in 1534 made the king head of the Church of England. Chief minister Thomas Cromwell closed the monasteries between 1536 and 1541.",
 why="The dynasty was young and needed an heir. The Pope was the Emperor's prisoner in all but name. Anticlerical feeling and Protestant ideas gave the king allies in Parliament.",
 trig="Anne Boleyn's pregnancy in early 1533 forced the marriage and the break.",
 then="Monastic land sold to the gentry gave a powerful class a stake in the settlement. England swung between Protestant and Catholic under Edward VI and Mary I until Elizabeth's settlement of 1559. Catholicism became identified with Spain and France.",
 fact="The Anglican Communion, grown from the church Henry took over, counts about 85 million members."),
"d-jesuits": dict(
 bluf="Basque soldier Ignatius of Loyola offered the Pope an order bound by obedience and trained for argument. Pope Paul III approved it in 1540. The Jesuits became the Counter-Reformation's main force: they ran the schools that educated Catholic elites, advised kings and carried the faith to Asia and the Americas.",
 now="The Jesuits made education a weapon of religious competition, and their schools formed Catholic ruling classes for four centuries. Their political reach got the order suppressed in 1773 and restored in 1814.",
 story="The Church needed educated priests to answer the Protestants. Basque soldier Ignatius of Loyola, converted after a battle wound, gathered companions in Paris. Pope Paul III approved the Society of Jesus on 27 September 1540. Members took a special vow of obedience to the Pope.",
 why="Rome needed disciplined, learned clergy. Ignatius supplied a military model of obedience and long training.",
 then="Jesuit schools educated Catholic elites across Europe. Francis Xavier reached India and Japan; Matteo Ricci reached the Chinese court. Catholic rulers expelled the order in the 1760s and the Pope suppressed it in 1773.",
 fact="Pope Francis, elected in 2013, was the first Jesuit pope."),
"eu154318": dict(
 bluf="Polish canon Nicolaus Copernicus wanted a simpler model that predicted the planets. He put the Sun at the centre and published in the year he died. His model contradicted ancient science and the Church's picture of the world, and the argument it started ran through Kepler and Galileo to Newton.",
 now="Copernicus began the conflict between measured evidence and received authority that defines modern science. The Church's ban of 1616 and its trial of Galileo in 1633 set the pattern of every later clash.",
 story="Polish canon Nicolaus Copernicus, a church administrator at Frombork, worked out a system in which the Earth and planets orbit the Sun. He held the manuscript back for years. 'On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres' was printed in Nuremberg in 1543, the year he died.",
 why="Ptolemy's Earth-centred system needed ever more adjustments. Greek texts recovered in the Renaissance offered alternatives, and the Church wanted an accurate calendar.",
 then="Few accepted the model at first; it predicted no better than Ptolemy. Kepler's ellipses and Galileo's telescope made the case. The Church banned Copernican works in 1616.",
 fact="The printer added an unsigned preface calling the system a mere calculating device, which protected the book for seventy years."),
"d-vesalius": dict(
 bluf="Padua professor Andreas Vesalius dissected bodies himself to check the ancient anatomy of Galen against the corpse. He found Galen had described animals, and published the correction with detailed woodcuts in 1543. Medicine became a science of observation, and the authority of ancient texts broke in the same year Copernicus moved the Earth.",
 now="Vesalius set the rule that observation outranks inherited authority, the principle behind evidence-based medicine. Padua trained the physicians who followed it, including William Harvey.",
 story="Galen's texts had governed medicine for 1,300 years. Padua professor Andreas Vesalius dissected human corpses with his own hands and published 'On the Fabric of the Human Body', seven illustrated books, in 1543.",
 why="Humanists recovering ancient texts were finding contradictions in them. Padua allowed dissection, and Venetian printers could reproduce fine woodcuts.",
 then="Anatomy became a dissection-based science. English physician William Harvey, trained at Padua, described the circulation of the blood in 1628.",
 fact="Vesalius left Padua for the court of Holy Roman Emperor Charles V the year the book appeared and never taught again."),
"eu154519": dict(
 bluf="Pope Paul III called a council to answer the Reformation. Over eighteen years the bishops reformed the abuses and conceded nothing on doctrine. The council fixed the line between Catholic and Protestant Europe and built the disciplined, centralised Church that lasted until the 1960s.",
 now="Trent made Catholic Europe a bloc with trained priests, a single liturgy and an Index. The Counter-Reformation it launched won back Poland, Austria and southern Germany and fixed the religious map of Europe.",
 story="Protestant gains in Germany, Scandinavia and England forced Rome to act. Pope Paul III opened the council at Trent in 1545; it met in three sessions until 1563. It condemned Protestant teachings, reaffirmed the seven sacraments and Church tradition, and ordered every diocese to open a seminary.",
 why="Complaints about corrupt and ignorant clergy were a century old. Rivalry between Pope and Emperor delayed the council for years.",
 then="The Latin Mass, the Vulgate Bible and the Index were confirmed. The Jesuits, the Inquisition and Baroque art carried the programme into central Europe.",
 fact="The Tridentine Mass fixed in 1570 was the Catholic liturgy until the Second Vatican Council of 1962-65."),
"eu155520": dict(
 bluf="Holy Roman Emperor Charles V had failed to crush the Lutheran princes, and his brother Ferdinand settled for a deal: each ruler would choose his territory's faith. The peace held for sixty years. It replaced the idea of one Christian truth with a political bargain and, by leaving Calvinists out, set up the Thirty Years' War.",
 now="Augsburg made religion a matter of territorial sovereignty. The rule 'whose realm, his religion' is the first step toward the system of sovereign states fixed at Westphalia in 1648.",
 story="Holy Roman Emperor Charles V had beaten the Lutheran princes in 1547 and lost the advantage to a princes' revolt in 1552. At the Diet of Augsburg in September 1555 his brother, Archduke Ferdinand, agreed that each ruler would choose Catholicism or Lutheranism for his lands. Subjects who disagreed could emigrate. Church lands seized before 1552 stayed seized.",
 why="Neither side could win. Charles needed peace in Germany to fight France and the Ottomans.",
 then="Religion became a function of the prince. Calvinism spread into territories the peace did not cover, and disputes over church lands ate at the settlement until war came in 1618.",
 fact="Charles V refused to sign the peace himself and abdicated the following year."),
"eu155821": dict(
 bluf="Elizabeth I inherited a divided, nearly bankrupt kingdom and set out to hold it together under a Protestant settlement broad enough to keep the peace. She reigned 45 years, kept England independent of Spain and never married. England came out a Protestant state turning toward the sea.",
 now="The Anglican settlement, the beginnings of English sea power and the East India Company date from the reign. Her refusal to marry passed the crown to the Scottish Stuarts and so to the union of 1603.",
 story="English Queen Mary I died childless in November 1558. Her half-sister Elizabeth I took the throne under Henry VIII's will, with William Cecil as chief minister. The religious settlement of 1559 created a Protestant church with Catholic forms.",
 why="Mary's Catholic restoration had died with her. Elizabeth's claim rested on the break with Rome, so her survival required a Protestant England.",
 then="She backed Dutch rebels and privateers against Spain, beat the Armada in 1588 and chartered the East India Company in 1600. The crown passed to Scottish King James VI in 1603.",
 fact="She used the question of her marriage as a diplomatic tool for twenty years."),
"eu156222": dict(
 bluf="French noble factions took up religion to fight for control of a crown held by boys. Eight civil wars over 36 years killed hundreds of thousands and ended only when a Protestant heir converted to take Paris. The wars taught France that a strong crown mattered more than religious unity, the lesson behind the absolutist state.",
 now="France's tradition of a strong central state and its suspicion of religion in public life descend from the memory of these wars. The settlement of 1598 was the first in Europe to tolerate two faiths by law.",
 story="French King Henry II died in a tournament in 1559, leaving young sons. Calvinism had spread among nobles and townsmen. The Guise, Bourbon and Montmorency families fought for control of the court and used religion to raise followers. Massacres were common on both sides.",
 why="A minority on the throne left no arbiter. Calvinism gave noble factions an army of believers. Spain and England fed money to the sides.",
 trig="Men of the Duke of Guise killed Protestant worshippers at Vassy in March 1562.",
 then="Henry of Navarre, the Protestant heir, became French King Henry IV, converted in 1593 and issued the Edict of Nantes in 1598. The 'politiques' argued that the state's survival outranked religious uniformity.",
 fact="The wars killed an estimated two to four million people, most by famine and disease."),
"eu156823": dict(
 bluf="Spanish King Philip II set out to tax the Low Countries hard and stamp out Protestantism there. The provinces rose under William of Orange, and after eighty years of war the seven northern ones won independence. The Dutch Republic became the world's leading trading power and the model of a commercial, tolerant, decentralised state.",
 now="The revolt split the Low Countries into the Netherlands and Belgium and created the Dutch Republic, whose stock exchange, public bank and chartered company are the tools of modern capitalism.",
 story="The Low Countries were the richest lands in Spanish King Philip II's empire. His taxes, his centralising officials and his persecution of Protestants alienated nobles and towns alike. The Duke of Alba executed leading nobles in 1568. Prince William of Orange led the armed revolt. The seven northern provinces declared independence in 1581.",
 why="Taxation came first, then the overriding of provincial privileges, then religion. The towns had money and the sea; Spain's armies had to march from Italy.",
 trig="Alba's Council of Troubles executed the Counts of Egmont and Hoorn in June 1568.",
 then="Spain recognised the republic in 1648. The south stayed Spanish and became Belgium. Amsterdam founded its exchange bank in 1609 and the VOC in 1602.",
 fact="Spain's army walked to the Netherlands along the Spanish Road through Savoy, Franche-Comté and Lorraine."),
"eu157124": dict(
 bluf="Pope Pius V assembled a Holy League of Spain and Venice to recover Cyprus from the Ottomans. The league destroyed the Ottoman fleet off Greece and gained nothing: the Ottomans rebuilt in a year, kept Cyprus, and Venice made peace in 1573. The battle ended Ottoman naval expansion in the western Mediterranean and little else.",
 now="Lepanto fixed the Mediterranean as a stalemate between two exhausted empires. Spain and the Ottomans turned to other fronts, and Europe's growth moved to the Atlantic.",
 story="The Ottomans had taken Venetian Cyprus in 1570. Pope Pius V formed a Holy League with Spanish King Philip II and Venice under Don John of Austria. About 200 galleys met on each side in the Gulf of Patras on 7 October 1571; the Ottomans lost most of theirs.",
 why="Venice wanted Cyprus back. Spain wanted its Italian coasts safe. The Pope wanted a crusade.",
 then="Venice signed a separate peace in 1573 and gave up Cyprus. Spain turned to the Netherlands, the Ottomans to Persia.",
 fact="Miguel de Cervantes, later author of Don Quixote, lost the use of his left hand in the battle."),
"eu157225": dict(
 bluf="French Queen Mother Catherine de' Medici and the Guise faction ordered the killing of Huguenot leaders gathered in Paris for a royal wedding, to end their influence over the young king. Mobs then killed thousands across France. The massacre destroyed any chance of compromise and taught Protestants to argue that subjects may resist a tyrant.",
 now="The resistance theories written after the massacre fed later ideas of limited and constitutional government. The religious wars ran 26 more years.",
 story="Huguenot nobles came to Paris in August 1572 for the wedding of Protestant Henry of Navarre to the king's sister. French Queen Mother Catherine de' Medici and the Guise faction, with French King Charles IX's assent, ordered the Huguenot leaders killed on 24 August. Admiral Gaspard de Coligny died first; Catholic mobs killed thousands in Paris and the provinces.",
 why="Catholic leaders feared Coligny's influence over the king and his push for war with Spain. The wedding had put the Huguenot leadership in one place.",
 then="Huguenot writers developed theories of resistance and limited monarchy. The wars continued until 1598.",
 fact="Pope Gregory XIII had a medal struck and a Te Deum sung to celebrate the news."),
"d-tycho": dict(
 bluf="Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe measured a new star in 1572 to see whether it lay below or beyond the Moon. It showed no parallax, so it lay among the fixed stars, where ancient doctrine said nothing could change. The result broke Aristotle's unchanging heavens, and Tycho's later measurements gave Kepler the data for the laws of planetary motion.",
 now="Tycho set the standard that theory must answer to precise measurement. The Danish crown's funding of his observatory was the first state investment in research.",
 story="On 11 November 1572 Danish astronomer Tycho Brahe saw a new star in Cassiopeia, at first brighter than Venus. His measurements showed it fixed among the background stars, far beyond the Moon. It was a supernova.",
 why="A rare exploding star appeared while an astronomer obsessed with precision was watching.",
 then="Danish King Frederick II funded Tycho's observatory on Hven. German astronomer Johannes Kepler inherited the data in 1601 and derived the laws of planetary motion.",
 fact="Tycho wore a metal nose after losing part of his own in a duel over a mathematical dispute."),
"d-maryqos": dict(
 bluf="English Queen Elizabeth I's ministers wanted the Catholic claimant removed before another plot succeeded. Mary, Queen of Scots, was tried for the Babington Plot and beheaded in 1587. Her death freed Spanish King Philip II to claim England for himself and cleared the way for her Protestant son to unite the crowns in 1603.",
 now="The union of crowns in 1603 led to the union of parliaments in 1707. The question of Scotland's place in the United Kingdom has run since.",
 story="Mary, Queen of Scots, had been held in England for nineteen years. Her letters approving the Babington Plot to assassinate Elizabeth were intercepted by Secretary Francis Walsingham. She was convicted and beheaded at Fotheringhay on 8 February 1587.",
 why="Catholic plotters, backed by Spain, treated Mary as Elizabeth's rightful heir. The intercepted letters gave the ministers their proof.",
 then="Philip II launched the Armada the next year claiming England for himself. Scottish King James VI became English King James I in 1603.",
 fact="Elizabeth signed the death warrant and then claimed it had been sent without her order."),
"d-cadiz": dict(
 bluf="English privateer Sir Francis Drake sailed to wreck Spanish King Philip II's invasion fleet before it could leave port. He burned dozens of ships and the seasoned barrel staves meant for the fleet's stores. The Armada sailed a year late with leaking casks, and the raid set the precedent for striking a fleet in harbour.",
 now="The raid delayed the Armada by a year and showed that a fleet could be destroyed in harbour before it sailed; pre-emptive naval strikes from Copenhagen in 1807 to Pearl Harbor follow its logic.",
 story="Spain was assembling an invasion fleet in 1587. English privateer Sir Francis Drake sailed into Cádiz harbour in April, destroyed some thirty ships and stores, and then burned thousands of seasoned barrel staves along the Portuguese coast.",
 why="Elizabeth I's government wanted Philip's invasion stopped before it was ready.",
 then="The Armada sailed in 1588 with casks of green wood that leaked and spoiled its food and water.",
 fact="Drake called the raid 'singeing the King of Spain's beard'."),
"eu158826": dict(
 bluf="Spanish King Philip II sent 130 ships to escort an army from the Netherlands to England, to restore Catholicism there and end English aid to the Dutch rebels. English fire ships and gunnery broke the fleet off Calais and storms wrecked much of it on the voyage home. England and the Dutch kept their independence, Spain's bid to control northern Europe failed, and English sea power began.",
 now="The defeat kept Protestant England and the Dutch Republic in being, which decided the religious map of northern Europe. The Armada became the founding story of British sea power.",
 story="In 1588 Spanish King Philip II sent about 130 ships up the Channel to carry the Duke of Parma's army across from Flanders. English ships harried the fleet, fire ships broke its formation off Calais and the battle of Gravelines scattered it. Storms wrecked dozens of ships on the voyage home round Scotland and Ireland.",
 why="Philip wanted Catholicism restored in England, English support for the Dutch ended and English raids on his treasure fleets stopped.",
 trig="The execution of Mary, Queen of Scots in 1587 ended Philip's hope of a Catholic successor and freed him to claim England himself.",
 then="The war ran until 1604 and Spain rebuilt its navy. England and the Dutch expanded into the Atlantic and Asia. Protestantism became the core of English identity.",
 fact="More Spanish ships were lost to storms off Scotland and Ireland than to English guns."),
"eu159827": dict(
 bluf="French King Henry IV needed Huguenot loyalty and Catholic acceptance to rebuild royal authority after 36 years of war. His edict kept Catholicism as the state religion and gave Protestants freedom of conscience, places of worship and 150 fortified towns. France got peace and a stronger crown; the edict held until a king who no longer needed it revoked it in 1685.",
 now="Nantes was the first European settlement to tolerate two faiths by law. Its revocation in 1685 drove 200,000 skilled Huguenots to France's rivals, the first modern case of persecution exporting an economy.",
 story="French King Henry IV, a Protestant who had converted to take Paris, issued the edict at Nantes in April 1598. Catholicism remained the state religion. Huguenots received freedom of conscience, worship in named places, and control of about 150 fortified towns.",
 why="Neither side could win. Henry needed both communities to govern.",
 then="Cardinal Richelieu took the Huguenot fortresses in 1628 as a state within the state. French King Louis XIV revoked the edict in 1685; about 200,000 Huguenots left for the Netherlands, England, Prussia and the Cape.",
 fact="Henry IV was assassinated by a Catholic fanatic in 1610."),
"eu160028": dict(
 bluf="London merchants sought a share of the Asian trade held by Portugal and the Dutch, and Elizabeth I gave them a monopoly. Beaten in the spice islands, the Company turned to India and over two centuries became its ruler, with an army, tax powers and a foreign policy. Britain's Asian empire was built by a corporation, and the British state took it over in 1858.",
 now="The Company is the clearest case of a corporation becoming a state. India's administrative system and its English-speaking elite descend from Company rule.",
 story="English Queen Elizabeth I granted a group of London merchants a monopoly on English trade east of the Cape in 1600. Outcompeted by the Dutch in the spice islands, the Company traded cotton, indigo and saltpetre from fortified posts at Surat, Madras, Bombay and Calcutta, and later Chinese tea.",
 why="English merchants wanted Asian goods without Portuguese or Dutch middlemen. A joint stock spread the risk of voyages that took years.",
 then="As Mughal power broke up after 1707, the Company's army fought French rivals and Indian states and won Bengal's revenues after Plassey in 1757. Parliament regulated it from 1773 and took India over after the rebellion of 1857.",
 fact="At its peak the Company's private army was about twice the size of the British Army."),
"eu160229": dict(
 bluf="Dutch merchants and the Dutch state wanted one company strong enough to beat Portugal in Asia and fund voyages that took years. The VOC got a monopoly, permanent capital, and the power to make war and treaties. It built the Dutch empire in Indonesia and, with shares anyone could trade, the modern stock market.",
 now="Tradable shares, the stock exchange and the chartered company descend from the VOC. Indonesia's borders follow the colonial state it began.",
 story="The Dutch Republic merged its rival Asian ventures into the Vereenigde Oostindische Compagnie in 1602. The company held a monopoly on Dutch trade in Asia and could build forts, sign treaties and wage war. Its capital was permanent, and investors sold their shares on the Amsterdam exchange.",
 why="Merchants wanted pooled capital for long voyages. The republic, at war with Spain, wanted a company that could fight the Iberians in Asia.",
 then="The VOC drove the Portuguese from much of Asia, founded Batavia in 1619, conquered the Banda Islands and monopolised nutmeg, cloves and cinnamon. Corrupt and indebted, it was dissolved in 1799 and the Dutch state took its territories.",
 fact="The VOC paid dividends averaging about 18 per cent a year for nearly two centuries."),
}
